2184 0 obj
  << /ActualText (8\056 NAVIGATION \046 WAYFINDING\n\nPrimary Navigation System \024 Red Emergency Light\nRed light is the level\047s core navigation tool\056 Players follow it instinctively without being told to\056 It is present at every key decision point and transition throughout the level\056\nThe rule is established explicitly in Beat 1 \024 a notice board reads\072 \042Follow red light for emergency exit\056\042 From that point the system operates silently\056\n\nDeliberate Navigation Disruption\nIn Beat 3 the red light is intentionally removed\056 The player experiences genuine disorientation \024 tight spaces\054 no clear direction\054 unfamiliar routes\056 When red light reappears\054 the psychological relief confirms how fully the player had internalised the navigation system\056\n\nExterior Orientation \024 Tower Labels\nAll four towers are labelled with large exterior signage visible from ground level and across the courtyard\056 Playtesting revealed players could not identify towers from outside despite the fax document referencing Tower Labels were added and orientation problems resolved immediately\056\n\nSecondary Wayfinding Tools\nCCTV monitors in Beat 5 show escape platforms ahead \024 rewarding curious players with tactical advance knowledge\nProximity monologues provide directional and narrative context at key transitions\nLighting contrast between indoor red emergency light and outdoor natural daylight maintains clear spatial orientation between interior and exterior spaces)
     /K [ 82 ]
     /P 2185 0 R
     /Pg 2020 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

